Given vector field, \[\mathbf{F}=-y\,\hat{i}+x\,\hat{j}\] and the closed curve \[r=1+\cos\theta\] where \[x=r\cos\theta,\qquad y=r\sin\theta\] The required line integral is \[\oint_{C}\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}\] Using Green's theorem, \[\oint_{C}(P\,dx+Q\,dy)
=
\iint_{A}\left(\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}\right)dA\] Here, \[P=-y,\qquad Q=x\] Therefore, \[\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}=1,
\qquad
\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}=-1\] Hence, \[\frac{\partial Q}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
=1-(-1)=2\] Thus, \[\oint_{C}\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
=
2\times(\text{Area enclosed})\] Area enclosed by the polar curve is \[A=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}r^{2}\,d\theta\] Substituting \[r=1+\cos\theta\] \[A=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}(1+\cos\theta)^{2}\,d\theta\] \[=\frac{1}{2}\int_{0}^{2\pi}
\left(1+2\cos\theta+\cos^{2}\theta\right)d\theta\] Using \[\int_{0}^{2\pi}\cos\theta\,d\theta=0,
\qquad
\int_{0}^{2\pi}\cos^{2}\theta\,d\theta=\pi\] we get \[A=\frac{1}{2}(2\pi+\pi)
=\frac{3\pi}{2}\] Therefore, \[\oint_{C}\mathbf{F}\cdot d\mathbf{r}
=
2\times\frac{3\pi}{2}
=3\pi\] \[3\pi \approx 9.42\] \[\boxed{9.42}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For a polar curve, \[A=\frac{1}{2}\int r^{2}\,d\theta\] gives the enclosed area.</li></ul></div>

Check each statement individually. <strong>Option A:</strong> False. Causality and stability are independent properties. A causal LTI system need not be BIBO stable. <strong>Option B:</strong> True. For a discrete-time LTI system, \[\text{System is causal}
\iff
h[n]=0,\quad n&lt;0,\] where \(h[n]\) is the response to the unit impulse input. <strong>Option C:</strong> False For stability, \[\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|h[n]|&lt;\infty.\] Consider a signal \[h[n]=\tan\left(\frac{n\pi}{4}\right), \qquad 0&lt;n&lt;4.\] which is of finite duration. Here, at \(n=2\), \[\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\] it is undefined (tends to \(\infty\)), which does not satisfy condition for BIBO stable system. <strong>Option D: False.</strong><br/>The condition \[0&lt;|h[n]|&lt;1,\qquad \forall n\] does not guarantee BIBO stability. For stability, the impulse response must satisfy \[\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|h[n]|&lt;\infty.\] A counterexample is \[h[n]=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{1}{n+1}, &amp; n\ge0,\\ 0, &amp; n&lt;0.
\end{cases}\] Clearly, \[0&lt;|h[n]|&lt;1,\qquad \forall n\ge0,\] but \[\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|h[n]|
=
\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n+1}
=\infty.\] Since the impulse response is not absolutely summable, the system is <strong>not</strong> BIBO stable. Hence, Option D is incorrect. \[\boxed{\text{(b) A discrete-time LTI system is causal if and only if its response to a step input $u[n]$ is $0$ for $n&lt;0$.}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The statement says that the impulse response is of finite duration. This means that \(h[n]\) is non-zero only over a finite number of samples and is a valid finite-valued sequence. Hence, \[\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}|h[n]|&lt;\infty,\] so the system is always BIBO stable. But if we consider examples such as \[h[n]=\tan\left(\frac{n\pi}{4}\right), \qquad 0&lt;n&lt;4.\] Here, at \(n=2\), \[\tan\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\] is undefined (tends to \(\infty\)). Such a sequence is not a valid finite-valued impulse response of an LTI system. But GATE official answer key mentions only Option B to be true, so that means they have considered the word 'finite valued' to be explicit which was not mentioned in question statement Hence, whenever GATE states that an impulse response has finite duration, it is implicitly assumed to be a well-defined finite-valued sequence. Therefore, Option C is not always <strong>true</strong>.</li></ul></div>

Using the inverse Fourier transform, \[x(t)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-W_0}^{W_0}e^{j\omega t}\,d\omega
=\frac{\sin(W_0t)}{\pi t}.\] \[x(0)=\lim_{t\to0}\frac{\sin(W_0t)}{\pi t}
=\frac{W_0}{\pi}.\] Hence, \(x(0)\) increases with \(W_0\), so option B is false. Also, as \(W_0\to\infty\), \[x(t)=\frac{\sin(W_0t)}{\pi t}\longrightarrow \delta(t),\] therefore option A is true. Now, at \[t=\frac{\pi}{2W_0},\] \[x\!\left(\frac{\pi}{2W_0}\right)
=\frac{\sin(\pi/2)}{\pi\left(\frac{\pi}{2W_0}\right)}
=\frac{2W_0}{\pi^2}\neq\pm\frac{1}{\pi}.\] Hence, both options C and D are false. Therefore, the correct statement is \[\boxed{\text{(a) }x(t)\text{ tends to be an impulse as }W_0\rightarrow\infty.}\]

For a periodic sequence of period \(N\), \[a_k=\frac{1}{N}X\!\left(\frac{2\pi k}{N}\right).\] Here, \[X(\Omega)=(1+\cos\Omega)e^{-j\Omega}, \qquad N=5.\] Hence, \[a_3=\frac{1}{5}X\!\left(\frac{6\pi}{5}\right)
=\frac{1}{5}\left(1+\cos\frac{6\pi}{5}\right)e^{-j\frac{6\pi}{5}}.\] Since \[\left|e^{-j\frac{6\pi}{5}}\right|=1,\] we get \[|a_3|
=\frac{1}{5}\left|1+\cos\frac{6\pi}{5}\right|.\] Now, \[\cos\frac{6\pi}{5}=-0.809017,\] therefore, \[|a_3|
=\frac{1-0.809017}{5}
=\frac{0.190983}{5}
=0.0381966.\] Rounded to three decimal places, \[\boxed{0.038}\]

The active power delivered to the receiving end of a transmission line is \[P_R=
\frac{V_SV_R}{|B|}
\cos(\beta-\delta)
-
\frac{|A|}{|B|}
|V_R|^2
\cos(\beta-\alpha)\] where <ul><li>\(\delta\) is the load angle,</li><li>\(\beta=\angle B\),</li><li>\(\alpha=\angle A\).</li></ul> For a given system, all quantities except \(\delta\) are constants. Hence, the receiving-end power is maximum when \[\beta-\delta=0\] or \[\boxed{\delta=\beta}\] For a nominal \(\pi\) transmission line, the ABCD constants are \[\begin{bmatrix}
A &amp; B\\
C &amp; D
\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
1+\dfrac{YZ}{2} &amp; Z\\ Y\left(1+\dfrac{YZ}{4}\right) &amp; 1+\dfrac{YZ}{2}
\end{bmatrix}\] Therefore, \[B=Z=R+j\omega L\] The phase angle of \(B\) is \[\beta
=
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{\omega L}{R}
\right)\] The total resistance and inductance of the transmission line are \[R
=
0.035~\Omega/\text{km}
\times
400~\text{km}
=
14~\Omega\] \[L
=
1~\text{mH}/\text{km}
\times
400~\text{km}
=
400~\text{mH}
=
0.4~\text{H}\] Substituting these values, \[\beta
=
\tan^{-1}
\left(
\frac{2\pi(50)(0.4)}
{14}
\right)
=
83.64^\circ\] Since the receiving-end power is maximum when \(\delta=\beta\), \[\boxed{\delta=83.64^\circ}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The maximum receiving-end power occurs when the load angle equals the phase angle of the transmission parameter \(B\).</li><li>For a nominal \(\pi\) model, \(B=Z\), so only the line impedance determines \(\beta\).</li><li>The angle of the impedance is obtained from \(\beta=\tan^{-1}\!\left(\dfrac{\omega L}{R}\right)\).</li></ul></div> <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not use the angle of \(A\) instead of the angle of \(B\) while applying the maximum power condition.</li><li>Remember to convert inductance from mH to H before substituting into \(\omega L\).</li><li>Use the total line resistance and inductance (after multiplying by the line length), not the per-kilometre values.</li></ul></div>

The objective is to achieve selective protection such that only the faulty section is isolated while minimizing the number of directional relays used. For faults on Line 1 and Line 2, fault current can flow in either direction because the two lines are connected in parallel between Bus 1 and Bus 2. Hence, ordinary overcurrent relays cannot distinguish the direction of fault current. Therefore: \[R_3 \text{ and } R_4\] must be directional overcurrent relays. These relays must block faults toward Line 1 and Line 2, respectively, to avoid unnecessary tripping. For Line 3 and the loads connected at Bus 2 and Bus 3, current flow is unidirectional from the source side toward the loads. Hence, directional relays are not required for: \[R_5,\;R_6,\;R_7,\;R_8\] Thus, the minimum number of directional relays required is only: \[R_3 \text{ and } R_4\] Therefore, the correct option is \[\boxed{
\shortstack[l]{
\text{(D)}\\
\text{R_3} and \text{R_4} are directional overcurrent relays blocking faults\\
towards Line~1 and Line~2, respectively.
}}\] <img src="img/cd24679130c9040a.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,420px)">

We are given a bar chart representing the discrete probability mass function \(P(x)\) of a random variable \(x\). We need to analyze the relative values of its mean, median, and mode. <img src="img/fccd8682fc993852.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,256px)"> We are given a bar chart representing a probability mass function \(P(x)\) for a discrete random variable \(x\). <strong>Step 1: Analyze the symmetry of the distribution</strong> <ul><li>Observing the plot, \(P(x)\) is perfectly symmetric about the vertical axis \(x = 0\) (i.e., \(P(-x) = P(x)\) for all \(x\)).</li><li>For any distribution that is symmetric about a point \(c\), the **mean** and **median** both equal \(c\).</li><li>Here, the center of symmetry is \(x = 0\), so: \[	ext{mean} = 0, \quad 	ext{median} = 0 \implies 	ext{mean} = 	ext{median}\]</li></ul> <strong>Step 2: Identify the mode(s) of the distribution</strong><br/><ul><li>The **mode** is the value of \(x\) at which the probability mass function \(P(x)\) reaches its maximum peak height.</li><li>Inspecting the histogram, the tallest bars occur at \(x = -13\) and \(x = 13\).</li><li>Thus, this is a bimodal distribution with modes at: \[	ext{mode} = -13 	ext{ and } 13\]</li><li>Since \(	ext{mode} 
e 0\), we have: \[	ext{mean} = 	ext{median} 
e 	ext{mode}\]</li></ul> This corresponds to choice A. \[ oxed{	ext{A. mean = median } 
e 	ext{ mode}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>For any distribution that is perfectly symmetric, the mean and the median are always exactly equal and sit at the center of symmetry. If the distribution is bimodal with its peaks located away from the center, the mode value will be different from the mean and median.</li></ul></div>
